a friendly match in the backyard of the  National Museum in the Yemeni city of  Aden the professional players from Al  Talal are taking on an amateur third  division side this dusty patch of land  is all the long-established Club has  left the stadium has been partly  destroyed and is now in a restricted  military area and occupied by soldiers  the league in Yemen stopped operating  when the war broke out  the only chance professional players get  to play football is in friendlies like  this the guys are not as fit as they  used to be because we don't train  regularly and because they have no  competitive games they only train three  or four times a month and the club only  pays them pocket money they struggle to  make ends meet just like the majority of  people in Yemen unemployed I was able to  live well from football but that's all  gone now I don't have enough anymore  it's really tough for me I have to think  about doing something else to earn money  maybe I'll join the army  there are many different factions  operating in southern Yemen with  different goals someone to separate  state in the south  others are fighting Houthi rebels in the  north the country is divided and  football is divided - there is only  informal contact with people in the  north of the country especially in those  areas that are controlled by the Houthi  rebels but we still have a joint  National Team works  but the national team has not played in  Yemen for years because of the security  situation it has to play all its matches  abroad Sampson in Aden is one of the  lucky sides to still have a stadium and  fans still watch the games even if they  are only friendlies I'm so happy to  watch the games I feel great to be here  with the young guys and to support them  Yemen's professional league may no  longer exist but the people's love of  football remains for many in Yemen  football provides a much-needed escape  from the brutal reality of civil war
 

